---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Dispatch from '../../components/Dispatch.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site.js';
---

<Base
  title="Why Pool Shock Isn't Working: The CYA Problem"
  description="If shocking your pool isn't clearing it, high stabilizer (CYA) is the most likely reason. What CYA does and the honest fix. Call (512) 764-9867."
>
  <h1>Why shocking your pool didn't work (the CYA problem)</h1>

  <p>
    If you've shocked a green pool more than once with no real change, the most likely cause
    isn't the amount of shock — it's stabilizer, also called CYA (cyanuric acid), built up so
    high that chlorine is chemically locked out of doing its job. More shock doesn't fix that. In
    fact, past a certain CYA level, no amount of chlorine can work, and the honest fix usually
    starts with removing water, not adding chemical.
  </p>

  <Dispatch problem="Shock treatment not clearing a green pool" area={SITE.serviceArea} />

  <h2>What CYA actually does</h2>
  <p>
    Stabilizer protects chlorine from breaking down too fast in direct sunlight — which matters
    in Texas, where UV would otherwise burn off chlorine within hours. Trichlor tabs, the most
    common chlorine source for automatic feeders, add stabilizer every time they add chlorine.
    Used for a few years without ever being diluted, CYA quietly climbs season after season,
    because it doesn't break down or evaporate — it only leaves the water when water leaves the
    pool.
  </p>

  <h2>Why high CYA disables chlorine</h2>
  <p>
    Think of CYA as sunscreen for chlorine: a little protects it, a lot blocks it from doing
    anything at all. At high CYA levels, the ratio of chlorine to stabilizer gets so lopsided
    that even a chlorine reading that looks fine on a test strip is barely sanitizing the water.
    This is the single most common reason we see a pool where the owner swears they "shocked it
    three times and it's still green."
  </p>

  <h2>How to tell if this is your problem</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      Your pool has used trichlor tabs as the primary chlorine source for more than a season or
      two without a partial drain in between.
    </li>
    <li>
      Chlorine reads present on a test but the water stays green, cloudy, or fights off algae
      poorly despite regular dosing.
    </li>
    <li>
      A CYA test (many basic kits skip this — it may need a dedicated test) comes back high
      relative to your chlorine level. As a rule of thumb, chlorine needs to stay well above a
      fraction of the CYA reading to actually sanitize; when CYA gets too high, that math stops
      working no matter how much chlorine gets added.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The honest fix</h2>
  <p>
    Because CYA only leaves through water leaving the pool, the fix is usually a partial drain
    and refill with fresh water to bring the ratio back into a workable range, followed by shock
    treatment that can finally do its job. Switching future chlorine source away from trichlor
    tabs (to unstabilized chlorine, for example) prevents the same buildup from recurring. This
    is exactly the kind of thing we test for before recommending anything — {SITE.feeLine}
  </p>

  <h2>When to call a pro</h2>
  <p>
    Testing your own chlorine and pH is fine to do yourself, but an accurate CYA reading and the
    decision of how much water to drain and refill is worth a professional visit — draining too
    much water too fast can be a problem for some pool shells (hydrostatic pressure), and getting
    the ratio right the first time saves you from a second round of wasted shock. If your pool
    fits the neglected-pool pattern more broadly, see our{' '}
    <a href="/guides/neglected-green-pool-recovery/">green-to-clean recovery guide</a>.
  </p>

  <section class="faq">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <h3>Can I lower CYA without draining any water?</h3>
    <p>
      Not practically for most homeowners — CYA reducers exist but are slow, expensive at scale,
      and less reliable than a partial drain and refill. Dilution remains the straightforward
      fix for most residential pools.
    </p>
    <h3>How much water actually needs to be drained?</h3>
    <p>
      It depends on how high the current CYA reading is and your target level — there's no
      universal percentage. Testing first is what turns this from a guess into a number.
    </p>
    <h3>Will switching away from trichlor tabs stop this from happening again?</h3>
    <p>
      It stops new stabilizer from being added with every dose, which is the root cause of the
      slow climb. It won't lower CYA that's already in the water — that still requires dilution —
      but it prevents the same problem from creeping back next season.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Dispatch problem="Shock treatment not clearing a green pool" area={SITE.serviceArea} />
</Base>
